1 NOTE Ahnentafel position 60 — father of Joaquim José Bohrer (P-0030), hence Celina's (P-0015) paternal grandfather. Married Rosa Eugenia de Lemos (P-0035); their marriage was justified and registered at the Nova Friburgo Matriz in 1879 (PAR-0002). Already deceased by 1890, when Alberto Bohrer's birth (CIV-0019) names him "Francisco José Bohrer já falecido".
1 NOTE Parentage and birthplace RESOLVED (1 August 2026) by his own registered will (PRB-0005, Provedoria de Nova Friburgo, registered 1888): he declares himself "natural deste termo" — i.e. born in the Nova Friburgo comarca — and "filho legitimo de Jacob Bahrer e de Catharina Mayer, já fallecidos" (parents P-0050, P-0051, family F-0024). Nationality is therefore Brazilian (a documented birthplace, not a surname inference); the earlier "immigrant vs Brazilian-born" question is settled — he was Brazilian-born, and the immigrant boundary moves up to his parents Jacob and Catharina. The will also names his wife and their four children (Joaquim, Laura, Guilherme, Fernando) as sole heirs and dates his death to c.1888 (consistent with "já falecido" by 1890).
1 NOTE Exact death date and cause now confirmed by the Imigrantes de Nova Friburgo compilation (PUB-0003, p.300, BORER I entry 1.1.4): born 04/07/1832, died 06/07/1888, causa mortis insuficiência mitral e dilatação aórtica. The couple lived at "Alto da Serra." The compilation also lists his siblings from Jacob Bohrer × Catharina Mayer/Moser: Catharina Borer n.24/07/1830 (⚭ Frederico Chaboudez), Maria Borer n.21/09/1834 (⚭ Francisco Xavier Chaboudez), Augusto Borer n.10/02/1837, and João José Borer n.10/09/1840 f.16/06/1859 (solteiro). Note: Joaquim José Bohrer (P-0030) is absent from the compilation's child list (it shows only Ludovina, Laura, Guilherme, Fernando); PRB-0005 (the will) is the authoritative source naming Joaquim as firstborn — the compiler likely missed his baptism record.
